; TEST: emit-exit=4
;
; `fma f64 %a %b %c` (ssa-ir.md 2.3): a*b+c fused into ONE rounding. The
; VERIFIER passes it (fma's rule is d_trivial, like every shape the parser
; routes through "F %a %b %c"); the EMITTER refuses it by name -- "fma:
; the SSE2 baseline has no fused multiply-add (FMA3 is a later
; extension)". Nothing in-tree produces one: `contractio explicita`
; means the only legal contraction is the one spelled `fma`, and the
; lowering never spells it. Exit 4 here, not 5, is the evidence the two
; layers disagree on purpose -- the same split reject_emit_straddle.ir
; records for a straddling bit field.
;
; The C twin refuses it too (a plain `a*b+c` in C is two roundings and
; stdargs has no fused form), so the differential phase's refusal parity
; holds: emit_c exits 4 on this same text.
;
; Mutation (run): the dispatcher's FMA arm pointed at the fadd lowering
; instead of the die -> emit_ir exits 0 and the check chain below runs
; without ever executing a fused anything -- silently answering a
; question the CPU was never asked. This fixture is what keeps that
; mutation loud.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = fconst f64 4609434218613702656
%2 = fconst f64 4611686018427387904
%3 = fconst f64 4615063718147915776
%4 = fma f64 %1 %2 %3
%5 = iconst u8 0
ret %5
}